{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Forms often edit data loaded from an API. A server-state library such as\nTanStack Query should own fetching, caching, loading, and retries; TanStack Form\nshould own the editable copy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query does not publish a separate Preact adapter. With\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@preact/preset-vite"},{"type":"text","value":", use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/react-query"},{"type":"text","value":" through Preact's compatibility\naliases, as shown below and in the repository example."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are two supported ways to provide async initial values: wait to create the\nform until the data is ready, or create it immediately with complete fallback\nvalues. Choose the approach that best matches the loading experience your UI\nneeds."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"wait-for-data-before-creating-the-editor","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wait for data before creating the editor"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render a loading or error state first, then mount a component whose\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultValues"},{"type":"text","value":" are complete."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query'\nimport { useForm } from '@tanstack/preact-form'\n\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n}\n\nexport function UserPage({ userId }: { userId: string }) {\n  const userQuery = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['user', userId],\n    queryFn: () => fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`).then((res) => res.json()),\n  })\n\n  if (userQuery.isPending) return <p>Loading…</p>\n  if (userQuery.isError) return <p role=\"alert\">Could not load the user.</p>\n\n  return <UserEditor key={userId} user={userQuery.data} />\n}\n\nfunction UserEditor({ user }: { user: User }) {\n  const form = useForm({\n    defaultValues: user,\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      await fetch('/api/users', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(value),\n      })\n    },\n  })\n\n  return <form>{/* fields */}</form>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This pattern is useful when no form state is needed during loading. Mounting the\neditor only after data exists also makes it explicit when switching records\nshould create a fresh form."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"create-the-form-before-data-arrives","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create the form before data arrives"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can instead call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useForm"},{"type":"text","value":" in the same component as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":". Provide a\nstatic, shape-complete fallback so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultValues"},{"type":"text","value":" are never "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const emptyUser: User = {\n  firstName: '',\n  lastName: '',\n}\n\nexport function UserPage({ userId }: { userId: string }) {\n  const userQuery = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['user', userId],\n    queryFn: () => fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`).then((res) => res.json()),\n  })\n\n  const form = useForm({\n    defaultValues: userQuery.data ?? emptyUser,\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      await fetch('/api/users', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(value),\n      })\n    },\n  })\n\n  if (userQuery.isPending) return <p>Loading…</p>\n  if (userQuery.isError) return <p role=\"alert\">Could not load the user.</p>\n\n  return <form>{/* fields */}</form>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When the query data replaces the fallback, TanStack Form applies the new\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultValues"},{"type":"text","value":" to untouched fields while preserving fields the user has\nalready touched. In this example, the fields remain unmounted during loading,\nso the user cannot edit them before the loaded values arrive. You can also\nrender the empty form immediately when that better suits the UI."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the approach demonstrated by the repository's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"examples/preact/query-integration"},{"type":"text","value":" example."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"background-refetches-and-record-changes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Background refetches and record changes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultValues"},{"type":"text","value":" establish the form's baseline; they are not a fully controlled\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"values"},{"type":"text","value":" prop. In the fallback pattern, later "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultValues"},{"type":"text","value":" updates use the\nsame touched-field-preserving behavior."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If background refetches arrive while a user is editing, decide at the product\nlevel whether to keep local edits, prompt before replacing them, or call\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.reset(nextValues)"},{"type":"text","value":" deliberately. Do not overwrite in-progress input merely\nbecause a query refreshed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When switching to a different record, either remount a keyed editor as in the\nfirst pattern or deliberately reset the existing form. Otherwise, touched\nvalues from the previous record can be preserved when the new defaults arrive."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"wait-for-data-before-creating-the-editor","text":"Wait for data before creating the editor","level":2},{"id":"create-the-form-before-data-arrives","text":"Create the form before data arrives","level":2},{"id":"background-refetches-and-record-changes","text":"Background refetches and record changes","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nForms often edit data loaded from an API. A server-state library such as\nTanStack Query should own fetching, caching, loading, and retries; TanStack Form\nshould own the editable copy.\n\nTanStack Query does not publish a separate Preact adapter. With\n`@preact/preset-vite`, use `@tanstack/react-query` through Preact's compatibility\naliases, as shown below and in the repository example.\n\nThere are two supported ways to provide async initial values: wait to create the\nform until the data is ready, or create it immediately with complete fallback\nvalues. Choose the approach that best matches the loading experience your UI\nneeds.\n\n## Wait for data before creating the editor\n\nRender a loading or error state first, then mount a component whose\n`defaultValues` are complete.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query'\nimport { useForm } from '@tanstack/preact-form'\n\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n}\n\nexport function UserPage({ userId }: { userId: string }) {\n  const userQuery = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['user', userId],\n    queryFn: () => fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`).then((res) => res.json()),\n  })\n\n  if (userQuery.isPending) return <p>Loading…</p>\n  if (userQuery.isError) return <p role=\"alert\">Could not load the user.</p>\n\n  return <UserEditor key={userId} user={userQuery.data} />\n}\n\nfunction UserEditor({ user }: { user: User }) {\n  const form = useForm({\n    defaultValues: user,\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      await fetch('/api/users', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(value),\n      })\n    },\n  })\n\n  return <form>{/* fields */}</form>\n}\n```\n\nThis pattern is useful when no form state is needed during loading. Mounting the\neditor only after data exists also makes it explicit when switching records\nshould create a fresh form.\n\n## Create the form before data arrives\n\nYou can instead call `useForm` in the same component as `useQuery`. Provide a\nstatic, shape-complete fallback so `defaultValues` are never `undefined`.\n\n```tsx\nconst emptyUser: User = {\n  firstName: '',\n  lastName: '',\n}\n\nexport function UserPage({ userId }: { userId: string }) {\n  const userQuery = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['user', userId],\n    queryFn: () => fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`).then((res) => res.json()),\n  })\n\n  const form = useForm({\n    defaultValues: userQuery.data ?? emptyUser,\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      await fetch('/api/users', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(value),\n      })\n    },\n  })\n\n  if (userQuery.isPending) return <p>Loading…</p>\n  if (userQuery.isError) return <p role=\"alert\">Could not load the user.</p>\n\n  return <form>{/* fields */}</form>\n}\n```\n\nWhen the query data replaces the fallback, TanStack Form applies the new\n`defaultValues` to untouched fields while preserving fields the user has\nalready touched. In this example, the fields remain unmounted during loading,\nso the user cannot edit them before the loaded values arrive. You can also\nrender the empty form immediately when that better suits the UI.\n\nThis is the approach demonstrated by the repository's\n`examples/preact/query-integration` example.\n\n## Background refetches and record changes\n\n`defaultValues` establish the form's baseline; they are not a fully controlled\n`values` prop. In the fallback pattern, later `defaultValues` updates use the\nsame touched-field-preserving behavior.\n\nIf background refetches arrive while a user is editing, decide at the product\nlevel whether to keep local edits, prompt before replacing them, or call\n`form.reset(nextValues)` deliberately. Do not overwrite in-progress input merely\nbecause a query refreshed.\n\nWhen switching to a different record, either remount a keyed editor as in the\nfirst pattern or deliberately reset the existing form. Otherwise, touched\nvalues from the previous record can be preserved when the new defaults arrive.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: async-initial-values\ntitle: Async Initial Values\n---\n\nForms often edit data loaded from an API. A server-state library such as\nTanStack Query should own fetching, caching, loading, and retries; TanStack Form\nshould own the editable copy.\n\nTanStack Query does not publish a separate Preact adapter. With\n`@preact/preset-vite`, use `@tanstack/react-query` through Preact's compatibility\naliases, as shown below and in the repository example.\n\nThere are two supported ways to provide async initial values: wait to create the\nform until the data is ready, or create it immediately with complete fallback\nvalues. Choose the approach that best matches the loading experience your UI\nneeds.\n\n## Wait for data before creating the editor\n\nRender a loading or error state first, then mount a component whose\n`defaultValues` are complete.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query'\nimport { useForm } from '@tanstack/preact-form'\n\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n}\n\nexport function UserPage({ userId }: { userId: string }) {\n  const userQuery = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['user', userId],\n    queryFn: () => fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`).then((res) => res.json()),\n  })\n\n  if (userQuery.isPending) return <p>Loading…</p>\n  if (userQuery.isError) return <p role=\"alert\">Could not load the user.</p>\n\n  return <UserEditor key={userId} user={userQuery.data} />\n}\n\nfunction UserEditor({ user }: { user: User }) {\n  const form = useForm({\n    defaultValues: user,\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      await fetch('/api/users', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(value),\n      })\n    },\n  })\n\n  return <form>{/* fields */}</form>\n}\n```\n\nThis pattern is useful when no form state is needed during loading. Mounting the\neditor only after data exists also makes it explicit when switching records\nshould create a fresh form.\n\n## Create the form before data arrives\n\nYou can instead call `useForm` in the same component as `useQuery`. Provide a\nstatic, shape-complete fallback so `defaultValues` are never `undefined`.\n\n```tsx\nconst emptyUser: User = {\n  firstName: '',\n  lastName: '',\n}\n\nexport function UserPage({ userId }: { userId: string }) {\n  const userQuery = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['user', userId],\n    queryFn: () => fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`).then((res) => res.json()),\n  })\n\n  const form = useForm({\n    defaultValues: userQuery.data ?? emptyUser,\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      await fetch('/api/users', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(value),\n      })\n    },\n  })\n\n  if (userQuery.isPending) return <p>Loading…</p>\n  if (userQuery.isError) return <p role=\"alert\">Could not load the user.</p>\n\n  return <form>{/* fields */}</form>\n}\n```\n\nWhen the query data replaces the fallback, TanStack Form applies the new\n`defaultValues` to untouched fields while preserving fields the user has\nalready touched. In this example, the fields remain unmounted during loading,\nso the user cannot edit them before the loaded values arrive. You can also\nrender the empty form immediately when that better suits the UI.\n\nThis is the approach demonstrated by the repository's\n`examples/preact/query-integration` example.\n\n## Background refetches and record changes\n\n`defaultValues` establish the form's baseline; they are not a fully controlled\n`values` prop. In the fallback pattern, later `defaultValues` updates use the\nsame touched-field-preserving behavior.\n\nIf background refetches arrive while a user is editing, decide at the product\nlevel whether to keep local edits, prompt before replacing them, or call\n`form.reset(nextValues)` deliberately. Do not overwrite in-progress input merely\nbecause a query refreshed.\n\nWhen switching to a different record, either remount a keyed editor as in the\nfirst pattern or deliberately reset the existing form. Otherwise, touched\nvalues from the previous record can be preserved when the new defaults arrive.\n"}
